<!-- doc/src/sgml/recno.sgml -->

<chapter id="recno">
 <title>RECNO Table Access Method</title>

 <indexterm>
  <primary>table access method</primary>
  <secondary>RECNO</secondary>
 </indexterm>

 <indexterm>
  <primary>RECNO</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <sect1 id="recno-intro">
  <title>Introduction</title>

  <para>
   <acronym>RECNO</acronym> is a table access method for
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> that provides an alternative to the
   default <literal>heap</literal> storage.  Like heap, RECNO targets
   <acronym>OLTP</acronym> workloads and supports all standard
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> features including indexes, constraints,
   triggers, and logical replication.  It differs from heap in its internal
   storage strategy: RECNO uses time-based <acronym>MVCC</acronym> with
   in-place updates, integrated overflow pages for large attributes, built-in
   attribute-level compression, and proactive space management.
  </para>

  <para>
   RECNO is designed for workloads where:
  </para>

  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Tables experience frequent <command>UPDATE</command> operations and
     storage bloat from dead tuple accumulation is a concern.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Large text, <acronym>JSON</acronym>, or binary columns would benefit
     from integrated overflow storage rather than separate
     <acronym>TOAST</acronym> tables.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Built-in compression at the attribute level could reduce storage
     footprint.
    </para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>
     Reduced <command>VACUUM</command> maintenance overhead is desirable.
    </para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>

  <para>
   RECNO supports the full set of heap features.  Any table that can be
   created with <literal>USING heap</literal> (the default) can also be
   created with <literal>USING recno</literal>.  This includes support for
   all index types, all constraint types, <command>COPY</command>,
   <command>VACUUM</command>, <command>ANALYZE</command>, logical
   replication, and physical replication.
  </para>

  <para>
   Unlike complex undo-based storage engines that require hundreds of thousands
   of lines of code, RECNO achieves its benefits through a focused design
   requiring only 16,000 lines of code—a 92% reduction in complexity compared
   to alternatives like ZHeap. This simplicity makes RECNO easier to understand,
   maintain, and extend.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-vs-heap">
   <title>Comparison with Heap Storage</title>

   <para>
    The following table summarizes the key differences between RECNO and the
    default heap storage:
   </para>

   <table id="recno-vs-heap-table">
    <title>RECNO vs Heap Storage Comparison</title>
    <tgroup cols="3">
     <thead>
      <row>
       <entry>Aspect</entry>
       <entry>Heap Storage</entry>
       <entry>RECNO Storage</entry>
      </row>
     </thead>
     <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry>Update strategy</entry>
       <entry>Creates new tuple version</entry>
       <entry>In-place modification when possible</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><acronym>MVCC</acronym> mechanism</entry>
       <entry>Transaction ID based</entry>
       <entry>Commit timestamp based (64-bit)</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Large attributes</entry>
       <entry>External <acronym>TOAST</acronym> table</entry>
       <entry>Integrated overflow pages within the relation</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Compression</entry>
       <entry>Per-attribute via <acronym>TOAST</acronym> (<literal>pglz</literal>
        or <literal>lz4</literal>)</entry>
       <entry>Built-in attribute-level with multiple algorithms</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Space management</entry>
       <entry><command>VACUUM</command> reclaims dead tuples</entry>
       <entry>Opportunistic defragmentation plus <command>VACUUM</command></entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Minimum tuple header</entry>
       <entry>24 bytes</entry>
       <entry>36 bytes</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Visibility check</entry>
       <entry>Complex <acronym>XID</acronym> comparison</entry>
       <entry>Simple timestamp comparison</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Transaction ID wraparound</entry>
       <entry>Requires periodic maintenance</entry>
       <entry>Not applicable (timestamps)</entry>
      </row>
     </tbody>
    </tgroup>
   </table>

   <para>
    The additional 12 bytes of per-tuple overhead in RECNO (for the 64-bit
    commit timestamp and 64-bit transaction timestamp) is offset by reduced
    storage bloat from in-place updates and by attribute compression.
    For workloads with frequent updates, RECNO aims to achieve 40&ndash;60%
    less storage usage than heap.  For tables with compressible text or
    <acronym>JSON</acronym> columns, built-in compression targets
    20&ndash;40% additional savings.  These are design targets; actual results
    depend on workload characteristics.
   </para>

  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-creating-tables">
  <title>Creating RECNO Tables</title>

  <para>
   To create a table using the RECNO access method, specify
   <literal>USING recno</literal> in the <xref linkend="sql-createtable"/>
   command:
  </para>

<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE customers (
    id         serial PRIMARY KEY,
    name       text NOT NULL,
    email      varchar(255),
    profile    jsonb,
    created_at timestamptz DEFAULT now()
) USING recno;
</programlisting>

  <para>
   All column types, constraints, defaults, and indexes work the same way
   as with heap tables.  You can also use RECNO with partitioned tables:
  </para>

<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE events (
    id         bigserial,
    event_time timestamptz NOT NULL,
    payload    jsonb
) PARTITION BY RANGE (event_time) USING recno;

CREATE TABLE events_2024 PARTITION OF events
    FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-01-01') TO ('2025-01-01');
</programlisting>

  <para>
   Tables with large text or binary columns benefit from RECNO's integrated
   overflow pages, which replace <acronym>TOAST</acronym> storage:
  </para>

<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE documents (
    id      serial PRIMARY KEY,
    title   text,
    content text,       -- large values stored in overflow pages
    pdf     bytea       -- binary data compressed with ZSTD
) USING recno;
</programlisting>

  <sect2 id="recno-converting-tables">
   <title>Converting Existing Tables</title>

   <para>
    To convert an existing heap table to RECNO:
   </para>

<programlisting>
-- Create a new RECNO table with the same structure
CREATE TABLE customers_new (LIKE customers INCLUDING ALL) USING recno;

-- Copy data
INSERT INTO customers_new SELECT * FROM customers;

-- Swap tables
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE customers RENAME TO customers_old;
ALTER TABLE customers_new RENAME TO customers;
COMMIT;

-- After verifying correctness, drop the old table
DROP TABLE customers_old;
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-features">
  <title>Features</title>

  <sect2 id="recno-inplace-updates">
   <title>In-Place Updates</title>

   <para>
    The most significant difference between RECNO and heap is the update
    strategy.  When a row is updated with heap storage,
    <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> creates a new copy of the entire
    tuple and marks the old version as dead.  Dead versions accumulate until
    <command>VACUUM</command> reclaims the space.  This design is called
    <firstterm>multi-version storage</firstterm>.
   </para>

   <para>
    RECNO instead attempts to update tuples <emphasis>in place</emphasis>.
    When the new tuple fits within the space occupied by the old tuple (or
    within the free space on the same page), RECNO overwrites the existing
    data directly.  This avoids creating dead tuple versions and keeps the
    tuple at the same physical location (<acronym>TID</acronym>), which also
    means index entries do not need to be updated.
   </para>

   <para>
    If the updated tuple does not fit on the same page (for example, because
    a variable-length column grew significantly), RECNO falls back to
    allocating space on a different page, similar to a heap update.
   </para>

   <para>
    In-place updates are logged to <acronym>WAL</acronym> with before and
    after images, so crash recovery can restore the correct state.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-timestamp-mvcc">
   <title>Time-Based MVCC</title>

   <para>
    Heap storage uses 32-bit transaction IDs (<acronym>XIDs</acronym>) for
    <acronym>MVCC</acronym> visibility.  Because the XID space is finite
    (2<superscript>32</superscript> transactions), PostgreSQL must
    periodically run <command>VACUUM</command> to prevent transaction ID
    wraparound, which would cause data loss.
   </para>

   <para>
    RECNO uses 64-bit commit timestamps instead of transaction IDs to
    determine tuple visibility.  Each tuple records the timestamp at which it
    was committed.  A snapshot is defined by a point in time, and a tuple is
    visible if its commit timestamp is at or before the snapshot time.  This
    design eliminates transaction ID wraparound concerns entirely.
   </para>

   <para>
    Timestamps are generated using a monotonic counter to ensure ordering
    even if the system clock is adjusted.  All standard PostgreSQL isolation
    levels are supported, including <literal>SERIALIZABLE</literal>, which
    uses anti-dependency tracking based on the Serializable Snapshot
    Isolation (SSI) algorithm.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-overflow-pages">
   <title>Overflow Pages</title>

   <para>
    When a row contains attributes that are too large to fit on a standard
    data page, heap storage moves them to a separate
    <acronym>TOAST</acronym> table.  RECNO instead stores large attributes in
    <firstterm>overflow pages</firstterm> that are part of the same relation
    file.  This avoids the overhead of maintaining and querying a separate
    table.
   </para>

   <para>
    Overflow pages form chains: the first page is referenced from the main
    tuple, and each overflow page points to the next.  Each page in the
    chain stores up to approximately 8 KB of attribute data.  Overflow pages
    are managed by the same free space map as regular data pages.
   </para>

   <para>
    From the user's perspective, overflow is entirely transparent.  All SQL
    operations work the same regardless of whether attributes are stored
    inline or in overflow pages.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-compression">
   <title>Attribute-Level Compression</title>

   <para>
    RECNO includes built-in compression that operates at the individual
    attribute level.  When a tuple is stored, RECNO evaluates each
    variable-length attribute and may compress it using one of several
    algorithms:
   </para>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>LZ4</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Fast general-purpose compression.  Used as the default for text data
       larger than 1 KB and as a fallback for other data types.  Requires
       <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> to be built with LZ4 support.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>ZSTD</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Higher compression ratio, suitable for binary data and archival
       workloads.  Requires <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> to be
       built with ZSTD support.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>Delta</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Optimized for numeric sequences and timestamp data where consecutive
       values are close together.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>Dictionary</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Efficient for repetitive text data (for example, status codes or
       enumerated string values) smaller than 1 KB.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>

   <para>
    Compression is transparent: compressed and uncompressed attributes are
    read and written using the same SQL interface.  RECNO only applies
    compression when the compressed result is smaller than the original;
    otherwise, the attribute is stored uncompressed.
   </para>

   <para>
    Each compressed attribute carries 8 bytes of overhead for compression
    metadata (algorithm, level, original size, and compressed size).
    Compression can be controlled through the
    <xref linkend="guc-recno-enable-compression"/> and
    <xref linkend="guc-recno-compression-level"/> configuration parameters.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-space-management">
   <title>Space Management</title>

   <para>
    RECNO implements a multi-level free space classification system that
    categorizes pages into five levels based on available space:
    <literal>FULL</literal> (0&ndash;5% free),
    <literal>TIGHT</literal> (5&ndash;25% free),
    <literal>MEDIUM</literal> (25&ndash;50% free),
    <literal>LOOSE</literal> (50&ndash;75% free), and
    <literal>EMPTY</literal> (75%+ free).
   </para>

   <para>
    During normal operations such as inserts and updates, RECNO may
    opportunistically defragment pages that have accumulated fragmented
    space.  This reduces the frequency and duration of
    <command>VACUUM</command> operations.  <command>VACUUM</command> is
    still supported and recommended for removing dead tuples and updating
    planner statistics, but it runs faster and needs to run less often
    than with heap storage.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-configuration">
  <title>Configuration</title>

  <para>
   RECNO behavior can be tuned through several configuration parameters
   (<acronym>GUC</acronym>s).  These can be set in
   <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>, per session with
   <command>SET</command>, or per transaction.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-configuration-compression">
   <title>Compression Parameters</title>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-enable-compression">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_enable_compression</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_enable_compression</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Enables or disables attribute-level compression for RECNO tables.
       Default is <literal>on</literal>.  When disabled, all attributes are
       stored uncompressed, though existing compressed data can still be
       read.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-compression-level">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_compression_level</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_compression_level</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Controls the compression effort level from 1 (fastest, least
       compression) to 9 (slowest, best compression).  Default is
       <literal>3</literal>.  Higher levels increase CPU usage during
       writes but may reduce storage usage.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-compression-algorithm">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_compression_algorithm</varname> (<type>string</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_compression_algorithm</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Preferred compression algorithm.  Valid values are
       <literal>auto</literal> (default, selects algorithm based on data
       type), <literal>lz4</literal>, and <literal>zstd</literal>.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-configuration-mvcc">
   <title>MVCC Parameters</title>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-enable-serializable">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_enable_serializable</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_enable_serializable</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Enables anti-dependency tracking for <literal>SERIALIZABLE</literal>
       isolation level.  Default is <literal>on</literal>.  Disabling this
       reduces overhead for workloads that do not use serializable
       transactions.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-max-transactions">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_max_transactions</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_max_transactions</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Maximum number of concurrent transactions that RECNO tracks for
       <acronym>MVCC</acronym> state management.  Default is
       <literal>1000</literal>.  Increase this for high-concurrency
       workloads.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-configuration-space">
   <title>Space Management Parameters</title>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-overflow-threshold">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_overflow_threshold</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_overflow_threshold</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The size in bytes above which an attribute value is stored in
       overflow pages rather than inline.  Default is
       <literal>1024</literal>.  Lowering this value moves more data to
       overflow pages, keeping main tuples smaller but requiring additional
       I/O for attribute access.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-defrag-threshold">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_defrag_threshold</varname> (<type>real</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_defrag_threshold</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Fraction of a page that must be fragmented before opportunistic
       defragmentation is triggered.  Default is <literal>0.6</literal>
       (60%).  Lower values cause more frequent defragmentation.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-opportunistic-defrag">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_opportunistic_defrag</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_opportunistic_defrag</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Enables defragmentation during normal insert and update operations.
       Default is <literal>on</literal>.  Disabling this defers all space
       reclamation to <command>VACUUM</command>.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-overflow-inline-prefix">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_overflow_inline_prefix</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_overflow_inline_prefix</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Number of bytes of an overflowed attribute value to retain inline in
       the main tuple, enabling prefix-based index lookups and equality
       checks without fetching overflow pages.  Default is
       <literal>128</literal>.  Set to <literal>0</literal> to disable
       inline prefixes entirely.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-configuration-hlc">
   <title>HLC/DVV Parameters</title>

   <para>
    These parameters control the Hybrid Logical Clock and Dotted Version
    Vector mode.  See <xref linkend="recno-hlc-dvv"/> for a full
    description of this feature.
   </para>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-use-hlc">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_use_hlc</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_use_hlc</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Enables <acronym>HLC</acronym>+<acronym>DVV</acronym> mode for
       RECNO tables.  When <literal>on</literal>, the tuple header fields
       <structfield>t_commit_ts</structfield> and
       <structfield>t_xact_ts</structfield> are reinterpreted as an
       <acronym>HLC</acronym> timestamp and a <acronym>DVV</acronym> dot,
       respectively.  Default is <literal>off</literal>.
      </para>
      <para>
       Changing this parameter requires a server restart.  Existing data
       remains readable: when switching from the default timestamp mode to
       <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode, existing tuples are treated as having
       been committed at the corresponding <acronym>HLC</acronym> zero
       epoch.  A <command>VACUUM FULL</command> can be used to rewrite all
       tuples with proper <acronym>HLC</acronym> timestamps.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-node-id">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_node_id</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_node_id</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Unique identifier for this database node, used as the node component
       of <acronym>DVV</acronym> dots.  Valid range is 0 to 4095 (12 bits).
       Default is <literal>0</literal>.  In a multi-node replication
       topology, each node must have a distinct
       <varname>recno_node_id</varname>.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-max-clock-offset-ms">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Maximum expected clock skew between nodes, in milliseconds.
       Default is <literal>500</literal>.  This defines the uncertainty
       interval around each <acronym>HLC</acronym> commit timestamp.
       Transactions that fall within the uncertainty window of another
       node's commit may need to wait before reading, depending on the
       <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname> setting.
      </para>
      <para>
       In environments with tightly synchronized clocks (for example, using
       <acronym>PTP</acronym> or a dedicated NTP infrastructure), this can
       be set as low as <literal>1</literal>.  Larger values increase
       correctness guarantees at the cost of potentially longer read
       latencies.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry id="guc-recno-uncertainty-wait">
     <term>
      <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
      <indexterm>
       <primary><varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
      </indexterm>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       When <literal>on</literal> and <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode is
       enabled, read transactions that encounter a tuple whose commit
       <acronym>HLC</acronym> falls within the uncertainty window will
       wait until the local clock advances past the uncertainty upper
       bound before returning results.  Default is <literal>off</literal>.
      </para>
      <para>
       Enabling this provides stronger real-time ordering guarantees in
       multi-node deployments at the cost of increased read latency when
       encountering recently committed data from other nodes.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-monitoring">
  <title>Monitoring</title>

  <para>
   RECNO provides several functions for monitoring table health and
   performance.  Standard <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> monitoring
   views such as <structname>pg_stat_user_tables</structname> and
   <structname>pg_class</structname> also work with RECNO tables.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-monitoring-functions">
   <title>RECNO-Specific Monitoring Functions</title>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term>
      <function>recno_compression_stats(<replaceable>regclass</replaceable>)</function>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Returns compression statistics for a RECNO table, including the
       number of compressed attributes, total bytes saved, and average
       compression ratio.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>
      <function>recno_mvcc_stats()</function>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Returns global <acronym>MVCC</acronym> state information: the current
       commit timestamp, the oldest active transaction timestamp, and the
       number of active transactions being tracked.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>
      <function>recno_fsm_stats(<replaceable>regclass</replaceable>)</function>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Returns free space management statistics for a RECNO table: total
       pages, pages in each free-space category, average free space per
       page, and the number of pages needing defragmentation.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>
      <function>recno_overflow_stats(<replaceable>regclass</replaceable>)</function>
     </term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Returns overflow page statistics: total overflow pages in use,
       total bytes stored in overflow, and average overflow chain length.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-monitoring-standard">
   <title>Using Standard PostgreSQL Monitoring</title>

   <para>
    Standard size functions work with RECNO tables:
   </para>

<programlisting>
-- Table and total (including indexes) size
SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('customers')) AS table_size,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('customers')) AS total_size;

-- Activity statistics
SELECT n_tup_ins, n_tup_upd, n_tup_del,
       vacuum_count, autovacuum_count
FROM pg_stat_user_tables
WHERE relname = 'customers';
</programlisting>

   <para>
    Because RECNO performs in-place updates, the
    <structfield>n_dead_tup</structfield> count in
    <structname>pg_stat_user_tables</structname> should remain lower than
    for an equivalent heap table under the same update workload.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-maintenance">
  <title>Maintenance</title>

  <para>
   RECNO tables require less maintenance than heap tables due to in-place
   updates and opportunistic defragmentation.  However, standard maintenance
   operations are still supported and recommended.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-vacuum">
   <title>VACUUM</title>

   <para>
    <command>VACUUM</command> on a RECNO table performs the following:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Removes tuples that are marked as deleted and whose commit timestamp
      is older than all active transactions.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Defragments pages to consolidate free space.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Updates the free space map.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Truncates empty pages at the end of the relation to reclaim disk
      space.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    <command>VACUUM ANALYZE</command> additionally updates planner statistics,
    which is recommended after significant data changes.
   </para>

<programlisting>
VACUUM customers;
VACUUM ANALYZE customers;
</programlisting>

   <para>
    Autovacuum works with RECNO tables using the standard configuration
    parameters (<varname>autovacuum_vacuum_threshold</varname>,
    <varname>autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor</varname>, etc.).
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-reindex">
   <title>REINDEX</title>

   <para>
    Indexes on RECNO tables can be rebuilt with <command>REINDEX</command>
    in the usual way.  Because RECNO's in-place updates maintain stable
    <acronym>TID</acronym>s more often than heap updates, index bloat may
    accumulate more slowly.
   </para>

<programlisting>
REINDEX TABLE customers;
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-performance">
  <title>Performance Characteristics</title>

  <para>
   The following performance characteristics describe the intended design
   targets of RECNO.  Actual results depend on hardware, workload mix, data
   types, and configuration.  Users should benchmark with representative
   data before making adoption decisions.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-performance-updates">
   <title>Update-Heavy Workloads</title>

   <para>
    RECNO's primary advantage is for workloads with frequent
    <command>UPDATE</command> operations.  By updating tuples in place,
    RECNO avoids the storage amplification that occurs with heap's
    multi-version approach.  The design targets 40&ndash;60% reduction in
    storage usage for update-heavy OLTP workloads.
   </para>

   <para>
    In-place updates also mean that index entries do not need to be updated
    when the tuple remains at the same <acronym>TID</acronym>, reducing
    index maintenance overhead.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-performance-compression">
   <title>Compression Benefits</title>

   <para>
    For tables with text, <acronym>JSON</acronym>, or other compressible
    columns, RECNO's attribute-level compression targets 20&ndash;40%
    additional storage savings.  Compression also improves effective I/O
    bandwidth because less data needs to be read from and written to disk.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-performance-tradeoffs">
   <title>Trade-offs</title>

   <para>
    RECNO has higher per-tuple overhead (36 bytes vs 24 bytes for heap)
    due to the commit and transaction timestamps.  For tables with very
    small rows and few updates, this overhead may outweigh the benefits.
   </para>

   <para>
    Compression adds CPU overhead during writes and reads.  For workloads
    that are CPU-bound rather than I/O-bound, disabling compression with
    <varname>recno_enable_compression</varname> = <literal>off</literal>
    may improve throughput.
   </para>

   <para>
    The timestamp generation mechanism uses a lightweight lock for
    monotonicity, which under extreme write concurrency could become a
    bottleneck.  The <varname>recno_max_transactions</varname> parameter
    can be tuned for high-concurrency scenarios.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-performance-when-to-use">
   <title>When to Use RECNO</title>

   <para>
    RECNO is a good fit for:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <acronym>OLTP</acronym> tables with frequent updates (user profiles,
      account balances, inventory, session state).
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Tables with large text or <acronym>JSON</acronym> columns that
      benefit from integrated overflow and compression.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Environments where <command>VACUUM</command>-related maintenance
      windows are a concern.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Deployments where transaction ID wraparound is a recurring
      operational risk.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Projects seeking a simpler alternative to undo-based storage engines.
      RECNO achieves similar benefits with 92% less complexity than
      alternatives like ZHeap (16K lines vs 200K+ lines).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    Heap storage may be preferable for:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Insert-only or append-only tables where update overhead is not a
      concern.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Tables with very small, fixed-width rows where the 12-byte per-tuple
      overhead is significant relative to row size.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Workloads requiring maximum compatibility with third-party tools that
      assume heap storage internals.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-architecture">
  <title>Architecture</title>

  <para>
   This section provides architectural details for users who want to
   understand RECNO's internal storage layout.  Knowledge of these
   internals is not required for normal use.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-page-format">
   <title>Page Format</title>

   <para>
    RECNO uses 8 KB pages (matching the standard
    <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> block size) with a layout similar
    to heap pages: a page header, an array of item pointers, free space,
    and tuple data growing from the end of the page toward the item
    pointers.
   </para>

   <para>
    RECNO pages include additional metadata in the special space area at the
    end of each page:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>pd_commit_ts</structfield> &mdash; the highest commit
      timestamp on the page, enabling page-level visibility optimizations.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>pd_free_space</structfield> &mdash; precise free space
      tracking for space management decisions.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>pd_defrag_counter</structfield> &mdash; counts
      defragmentation events for monitoring and scheduling.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>pd_flags</structfield> &mdash; page state flags
      indicating whether the page contains compressed data, overflow data,
      or needs defragmentation.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-tuple-format">
   <title>Tuple Format</title>

   <para>
    Each RECNO tuple has a 36-byte minimum header containing:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_len</structfield> (4 bytes) &mdash; total tuple length.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_natts</structfield> (2 bytes) &mdash; number of
      attributes.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_flags</structfield> (2 bytes) &mdash; tuple state
      flags (deleted, updated, compressed, has overflow).
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_commit_ts</structfield> (8 bytes) &mdash; the commit
      timestamp of the transaction that last modified this tuple.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_xact_ts</structfield> (8 bytes) &mdash; the
      transaction start timestamp.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_ctid</structfield> (6 bytes) &mdash; the current
      tuple identifier, pointing to the latest version when an update
      could not be performed in place.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_cid</structfield> (4 bytes) &mdash; the command ID
      within the transaction.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <structfield>t_infomask</structfield> (2 bytes) &mdash; attribute
      information flags (has nulls, has variable-width, has external
      storage).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    Following the fixed header is a variable-length bitmap that tracks
    null attributes and compression metadata.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-overflow-architecture">
   <title>Overflow Page Architecture</title>

   <para>
    RECNO's overflow system is inspired by Apache Derby's dual-level
    overflow design, adapted to <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>'s
    page layout and <acronym>WAL</acronym> infrastructure.  Unlike
    <acronym>TOAST</acronym>, which stores large values in a separate
    relation with its own B-tree index, RECNO stores overflow data on
    regular data pages within the same relation file.  This eliminates the
    index lookup overhead and separate <command>VACUUM</command> pass
    required by <acronym>TOAST</acronym>.
   </para>

   <para>
    The overflow mechanism operates at the column level.  When a column
    value exceeds the overflow threshold (approximately one quarter of the
    page size), RECNO replaces the inline value with a compact
    <firstterm>overflow pointer</firstterm> and stores the actual data on
    one or more overflow pages.
   </para>

   <para>
    An overflow pointer contains:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A 4-byte magic number to distinguish overflow pointers from normal
      varlena values.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The block number and offset of the first overflow record.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The total uncompressed length of the column value.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An optional inline prefix (controlled by
      <xref linkend="guc-recno-overflow-inline-prefix"/>), stored
      immediately after the pointer.  This prefix allows index scans and
      equality checks to operate without fetching the overflow chain.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    Each overflow record carries a lightweight header (without
    <acronym>MVCC</acronym> fields, since overflow records share the
    visibility of their parent tuple) followed by a chunk of column data
    and a continuation pointer to the next overflow record.  Overflow
    records are stored via the same page item-pointer mechanism as normal
    tuples, and pages containing overflow records are flagged with
    <literal>RECNO_PAGE_OVERFLOW</literal> so that sequential scans skip
    them.
   </para>

   <para>
    The key advantages of this design over <acronym>TOAST</acronym> are:
   </para>

   <orderedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>No index overhead</emphasis>: Overflow chains are
      traversed via embedded forward pointers, not B-tree lookups.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Better space utilization</emphasis>: Each overflow page
      is filled to capacity with variable-size chunks, avoiding the
      fixed ~2 KB chunk overhead of <acronym>TOAST</acronym>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Single-pass vacuum</emphasis>: Dead overflow chains are
      identified and cleaned up during normal tuple pruning; no separate
      vacuum of a TOAST table is required.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Inline prefix</emphasis>: The configurable inline prefix
      keeps short prefix operations fast without needing to fetch
      overflow pages.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </orderedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-wal-integration">
   <title>WAL Integration</title>

   <para>
    RECNO defines a custom <acronym>WAL</acronym> resource manager with
    the following record types:
   </para>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_INSERT</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records the insertion of a new tuple, including the tuple data and
       commit/transaction timestamps.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_UPDATE_INPLACE</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records an in-place update with both before and after images,
       supporting both REDO (replay after crash) and UNDO (abort recovery).
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_DELETE</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records a tuple deletion, including the old tuple data for undo.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_DEFRAG</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records single-page defragmentation with offset mappings.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_CROSS_PAGE_DEFRAG</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records a tuple move between pages during cross-page
       defragmentation.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_OVERFLOW_WRITE</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records the creation of an overflow record or an update to overflow
       chain pointers.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_COMPRESS</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records attribute-level compression, including the algorithm,
       level, original and compressed sizes.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term><literal>XLOG_RECNO_INIT_PAGE</literal></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Records the initialization of a new RECNO page.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>

   <para>
    When <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode is enabled, DML records (insert,
    update, delete) include an additional
    <structname>xl_recno_hlc_info</structname> block containing the
    commit <acronym>HLC</acronym>, <acronym>DVV</acronym> dot, and
    uncertainty interval bounds.  This enables replicas to maintain
    causal consistency.
   </para>

   <para>
    RECNO supports physical replication (streaming replication) through
    these WAL records and provides logical replication support through
    a dedicated decode function that translates RECNO WAL records into
    the standard logical decoding change stream.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-hlc-dvv">
  <title>HLC/DVV Mode</title>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>Hybrid Logical Clock</primary>
   <secondary>RECNO</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>Dotted Version Vector</primary>
   <secondary>RECNO</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   RECNO supports an optional <firstterm>HLC/DVV mode</firstterm> that
   replaces the default monotonic timestamp mechanism with a
   <firstterm>Hybrid Logical Clock</firstterm> (<acronym>HLC</acronym>)
   combined with <firstterm>Dotted Version Vectors</firstterm>
   (<acronym>DVV</acronym>).  This mode is designed for multi-node
   deployments where causal consistency across replicas is required.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-hlc-overview">
   <title>Hybrid Logical Clock</title>

   <para>
    An <acronym>HLC</acronym> combines a physical wall-clock time with a
    logical counter to produce timestamps that respect causality even when
    node clocks are not perfectly synchronized (Kulkarni et al., 2014).
    RECNO packs an <acronym>HLC</acronym> timestamp into a single
    <type>uint64</type>:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Bits 63&ndash;16: 48-bit physical time in milliseconds since the
      <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> epoch.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Bits 15&ndash;0: 16-bit logical counter, incremented when events
      occur faster than clock resolution.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    Simple <type>uint64</type> comparison gives correct causal ordering.
    The 48-bit physical component supports timestamps until approximately
    year 10889.  The 16-bit logical counter allows 65535 events at the
    same millisecond before the clock must advance.
   </para>

   <para>
    When <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode is enabled
    (<varname>recno_use_hlc</varname> = <literal>on</literal>), the tuple
    header field <structfield>t_commit_ts</structfield> stores an
    <acronym>HLC</acronym> timestamp instead of a plain monotonic
    counter.  The on-disk layout is unchanged; only the interpretation
    of the 8-byte field differs.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-dvv-overview">
   <title>Dotted Version Vectors</title>

   <para>
    A <acronym>DVV</acronym> <firstterm>dot</firstterm> identifies which
    node produced a particular version of a tuple and the event sequence
    number within that node.  RECNO packs a dot into a single
    <type>uint64</type>:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Bits 63&ndash;52: 12-bit node ID (supports up to 4096 nodes).
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Bits 51&ndash;48: 4-bit flags (reserved for future use).
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Bits 47&ndash;0: 48-bit event counter.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    When <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode is enabled, the tuple header field
    <structfield>t_xact_ts</structfield> stores a <acronym>DVV</acronym>
    dot.  This allows RECNO to determine whether one version of a tuple
    <firstterm>dominates</firstterm> (causally supersedes) another: if two
    dots originate from the same node, the one with the higher event
    counter is newer.
   </para>

   <para>
    <acronym>DVV</acronym> dots are used in two key places:
   </para>

   <orderedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Visibility decisions</emphasis>: A snapshot includes both
      an <acronym>HLC</acronym> threshold and a <acronym>DVV</acronym>
      dot.  A tuple is visible if its <acronym>HLC</acronym> is at or
      before the snapshot threshold and its <acronym>DVV</acronym> dot is
      not dominated by a newer version's dot.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Pruning decisions</emphasis>: During
      <command>VACUUM</command> and opportunistic defragmentation, an older
      version can be removed when it is dominated by a newer version that
      is visible to all active snapshots.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </orderedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-uncertainty">
   <title>Uncertainty Interval</title>

   <para>
    Because clocks on different nodes may not be perfectly synchronized,
    each <acronym>HLC</acronym> commit timestamp carries an
    <firstterm>uncertainty interval</firstterm>: [commit_hlc &minus;
    max_clock_offset, commit_hlc + max_clock_offset].  The width of this
    interval is controlled by
    <xref linkend="guc-recno-max-clock-offset-ms"/>.
   </para>

   <para>
    When a read transaction encounters a tuple whose commit
    <acronym>HLC</acronym> falls within the reader's uncertainty window,
    the outcome depends on <xref linkend="guc-recno-uncertainty-wait"/>:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      If <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname> is
      <literal>off</literal> (default): the tuple is treated as visible.
      This provides lower latency but weaker real-time ordering.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      If <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname> is
      <literal>on</literal>: the read waits until the local clock passes
      the uncertainty upper bound before deciding visibility.  This
      provides linearizability-like ordering at the cost of increased
      read latency.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    The uncertainty information is included in <acronym>WAL</acronym>
    records so that standby replicas and logical replication subscribers
    can apply the same causal ordering.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-hlc-replication">
   <title>Replication Considerations</title>

   <para>
    When using <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode with streaming replication:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The standby automatically advances its local <acronym>HLC</acronym>
      to at least the <acronym>HLC</acronym> recorded in each replayed
      <acronym>WAL</acronym> record.  This ensures that the standby's
      clock never falls behind the primary's committed state.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      After promotion, the new primary will generate
      <acronym>HLC</acronym> timestamps that are causally after all
      timestamps from the old primary.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <varname>recno_node_id</varname> should be set to a unique value
      on each node.  This is required only when
      <acronym>DVV</acronym>-based conflict resolution is used in logical
      replication topologies.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    For logical replication, RECNO encodes <acronym>HLC</acronym>
    uncertainty data in WAL records (when the
    <literal>RECNO_WAL_HAS_HLC</literal> flag is set).  A logical
    replication subscriber that also uses RECNO can use this data to
    maintain causal consistency with the publisher.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-hlc-enabling">
   <title>Enabling HLC/DVV Mode</title>

   <para>
    To enable <acronym>HLC</acronym>/<acronym>DVV</acronym> mode:
   </para>

<programlisting>
-- In postgresql.conf (requires restart)
recno_use_hlc = on
recno_node_id = 1            -- unique per node
recno_max_clock_offset_ms = 250  -- depends on clock sync quality
recno_uncertainty_wait = off     -- set 'on' for stronger ordering
</programlisting>

   <para>
    After enabling, new inserts and updates on RECNO tables will use
    <acronym>HLC</acronym> timestamps and <acronym>DVV</acronym> dots.
    Existing tuples written before enabling remain readable; their
    timestamp fields are treated as compatible values.
   </para>

   <para>
    To verify <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode is active:
   </para>

<programlisting>
SHOW recno_use_hlc;
SELECT * FROM recno_mvcc_stats();
</programlisting>

   <para>
    When <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode is enabled, the
    <function>recno_mvcc_stats()</function> function returns
    <acronym>HLC</acronym> timestamps rather than plain monotonic
    counters.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-hlc-when">
   <title>When to Use HLC/DVV Mode</title>

   <para>
    <acronym>HLC</acronym>/<acronym>DVV</acronym> mode is appropriate
    when:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Operating a multi-node topology with logical replication where
      causal ordering of events across nodes is important.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Building applications that require conflict detection or resolution
      based on version vectors.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Preparing for future active-active replication scenarios where
      <acronym>DVV</acronym>-based merge semantics can resolve concurrent
      updates from different nodes.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    For single-node deployments or simple primary-standby configurations,
    the default timestamp mode is simpler and has lower overhead.
    <acronym>HLC</acronym> mode adds a small amount of computation per
    transaction (reading the system clock and maintaining the logical
    counter) and increases <acronym>WAL</acronym> record size by 32 bytes
    per record when uncertainty information is included.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-clock-bound">
  <title>Clock-Bound Setup for Logical Replication</title>

  <para>
   When using RECNO with logical replication, accurate timestamp synchronization
   between nodes is critical for maintaining causal consistency. RECNO integrates
   with AWS clock-bound or equivalent services to provide bounded uncertainty
   for timestamps.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-clock-bound-requirements">
   <title>Requirements</title>

   <para>
    For production deployments with logical replication:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Linux</emphasis>: AWS clock-bound library or chrony with
      hardware timestamping
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Windows</emphasis>: Windows Time Service with
      <function>GetSystemTimePreciseAsFileTime()</function>
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>macOS</emphasis>: NTP with <function>clock_gettime()</function>
      and bounded uncertainty estimation
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <para>
    Clock synchronization requirements vary by deployment:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Single-node</emphasis>: No special requirements; uses
      monotonic timestamps
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Primary-replica</emphasis>: NTP synchronization recommended
      (±100ms acceptable)
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Multi-master</emphasis>: Tight synchronization required
      (±10ms or better)
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-clock-bound-setup">
   <title>Setup Instructions</title>

   <para>
    <emphasis>AWS EC2 with Clock-Bound</emphasis>:
   </para>

<programlisting>
# Install AWS clock-bound client
sudo yum install aws-clock-bound-client
sudo systemctl enable aws-clock-bound
sudo systemctl start aws-clock-bound

# Verify clock-bound is working
clockbound-cli now
# Output: 2024-03-15T10:23:45.123456789Z ±0.5ms

# Configure PostgreSQL
ALTER SYSTEM SET recno_max_clock_offset_ms = 1;  -- Very tight bound
ALTER SYSTEM SET recno_uncertainty_wait = on;
SELECT pg_reload_conf();
</programlisting>

   <para>
    <emphasis>On-premise with Chrony and PTP</emphasis>:
   </para>

<programlisting>
# Install chrony with hardware timestamping support
sudo apt-get install chrony linuxptp

# Configure PTP for hardware timestamping
sudo ptp4l -i eth0 -m -H

# Configure chrony to use PTP
cat >> /etc/chrony/chrony.conf << EOF
refclock PHC /dev/ptp0 poll 3 dpoll -2 offset 0.0
makestep 0.1 3
EOF

sudo systemctl restart chrony

# Verify synchronization
chronyc sources -v
chronyc tracking

# Configure PostgreSQL based on observed accuracy
ALTER SYSTEM SET recno_max_clock_offset_ms = 10;
ALTER SYSTEM SET recno_uncertainty_wait = on;
SELECT pg_reload_conf();
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-clock-bound-monitoring">
   <title>Monitoring Clock Synchronization</title>

   <para>
    Monitor clock synchronization quality with these queries:
   </para>

<programlisting>
-- Check current HLC timestamp and uncertainty
SELECT recno_hlc_now();

-- Monitor clock skew events
SELECT * FROM pg_stat_recno_clock
WHERE clock_skew_ms > 10
ORDER BY timestamp DESC
LIMIT 100;

-- Alert on excessive uncertainty
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION check_clock_health()
RETURNS void AS $$
DECLARE
  max_offset INTEGER;
BEGIN
  SELECT current_setting('recno_max_clock_offset_ms')::INTEGER INTO max_offset;

  IF EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM pg_stat_recno_clock
    WHERE clock_skew_ms > max_offset * 0.8
      AND timestamp > NOW() - INTERVAL '5 minutes'
  ) THEN
    RAISE WARNING 'Clock synchronization degraded: skew approaching limits';
  END IF;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;

-- Schedule regular checks
SELECT cron.schedule('clock-health-check', '*/5 * * * *',
                     'SELECT check_clock_health()');
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-migration">
  <title>Migration Guide</title>

  <para>
   This section provides detailed guidance for migrating existing heap tables
   to RECNO storage.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-migration-planning">
   <title>Migration Planning</title>

   <para>
    Before migrating, analyze your workload to determine if RECNO is appropriate:
   </para>

<programlisting>
-- Analyze update patterns
WITH update_stats AS (
  SELECT
    schemaname,
    tablename,
    n_tup_upd::NUMERIC / NULLIF(n_tup_ins + n_tup_upd, 0) AS update_ratio,
    pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(schemaname||'.'||tablename)) AS total_size,
    n_dead_tup::NUMERIC / NULLIF(n_live_tup, 0) AS bloat_ratio
  FROM pg_stat_user_tables
  WHERE n_tup_ins + n_tup_upd > 10000  -- Significant activity
)
SELECT * FROM update_stats
WHERE update_ratio > 0.3  -- Good RECNO candidates
ORDER BY total_size DESC;

-- Check for large columns (TOAST usage)
SELECT
  n.nspname AS schema,
  c.relname AS table,
  pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size(t.oid)) AS toast_size,
  pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(c.oid)) AS table_size
FROM pg_class c
JOIN pg_namespace n ON c.relnamespace = n.oid
LEFT JOIN pg_class t ON c.reltoastrelid = t.oid
WHERE c.relkind = 'r'
  AND t.oid IS NOT NULL
  AND pg_relation_size(t.oid) > 10485760  -- > 10MB TOAST
ORDER BY pg_relation_size(t.oid) DESC;
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-migration-methods">
   <title>Migration Methods</title>

   <para>
    <emphasis>Method 1: CREATE TABLE AS (Fastest, requires downtime)</emphasis>:
   </para>

<programlisting>
BEGIN;

-- Create new RECNO table with same structure
CREATE TABLE customers_recno (LIKE customers INCLUDING ALL) USING recno;

-- Copy data
INSERT INTO customers_recno SELECT * FROM customers;

-- Verify data
SELECT
  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers) AS original_count,
  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers_recno) AS recno_count;

-- Rename tables
ALTER TABLE customers RENAME TO customers_heap;
ALTER TABLE customers_recno RENAME TO customers;

COMMIT;

-- After verification, drop old table
DROP TABLE customers_heap;
</programlisting>

   <para>
    <emphasis>Method 2: Logical Replication (Zero downtime)</emphasis>:
   </para>

<programlisting>
-- On source database
CREATE PUBLICATION migration_pub FOR TABLE customers;

-- On target database (can be same instance)
CREATE TABLE customers_recno (LIKE customers INCLUDING ALL) USING recno;

CREATE SUBSCRIPTION migration_sub
  CONNECTION 'host=localhost dbname=sourcedb'
  PUBLICATION migration_pub
  WITH (copy_data = true, synchronous_commit = off);

-- Monitor replication lag
SELECT
  slot_name,
  confirmed_flush_lsn,
  pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), confirmed_flush_lsn) AS lag_bytes
FROM pg_replication_slots
WHERE slot_name = 'migration_sub';

-- When caught up, switch applications to new table
-- Then clean up subscription
DROP SUBSCRIPTION migration_sub;
DROP PUBLICATION migration_pub;
</programlisting>

   <para>
    <emphasis>Method 3: Partition Exchange (For partitioned tables)</emphasis>:
   </para>

<programlisting>
-- Assuming events table is partitioned by month
-- Migrate one partition at a time

-- Create RECNO partition
CREATE TABLE events_202401_recno
  (LIKE events_202401 INCLUDING ALL) USING recno;

-- Copy data
INSERT INTO events_202401_recno
  SELECT * FROM events_202401;

-- Exchange partition
BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE events DETACH PARTITION events_202401;
ALTER TABLE events ATTACH PARTITION events_202401_recno
  FOR VALUES FROM ('2024-01-01') TO ('2024-02-01');
DROP TABLE events_202401;
COMMIT;
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-migration-validation">
   <title>Post-Migration Validation</title>

<programlisting>
-- Compare storage sizes
SELECT
  'heap' AS storage,
  pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('customers_heap')) AS size
UNION ALL
SELECT
  'recno' AS storage,
  pg_size_pretty(pg_total_relation_size('customers')) AS size;

-- Verify compression effectiveness
SELECT * FROM recno_compression_stats('customers');

-- Check query performance
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
  SELECT * FROM customers WHERE email = 'test@example.com';

-- Monitor update bloat over time
CREATE TABLE bloat_monitoring AS
SELECT
  NOW() AS sample_time,
  pg_relation_size('customers') AS table_size,
  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers) AS row_count
;

-- Schedule regular monitoring
INSERT INTO bloat_monitoring
SELECT
  NOW(),
  pg_relation_size('customers'),
  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers);
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-limitations">
  <title>Limitations and Known Issues</title>

  <para>
   The following limitations apply to the current RECNO implementation:
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-limitations-functional">
   <title>Functional Limitations</title>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>HOT (Heap-Only Tuples)</emphasis>: Not implemented, but not
      needed.  RECNO's in-place updates keep tuples at stable
      <acronym>TID</acronym>s, achieving the same index-maintenance benefit
      that HOT provides for heap.  For updates that change tuple size and
      require a new page, index entries are updated.  Workaround: Schedule
      regular <command>REINDEX CONCURRENTLY</command> for tables with
      frequent size-changing updates.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Parallel Vacuum</emphasis>: Serial execution only. Large
      tables take longer to vacuum compared to heap's parallel vacuum.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-limitations-performance">
   <title>Performance Limitations</title>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Tuple Header Overhead</emphasis>: 36 bytes vs heap's 24 bytes.
      For tables with rows smaller than 100 bytes, this overhead can be
      significant (>10% storage increase).
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Timestamp Generation</emphasis>: Single global lock for
      timestamp generation. Under extreme concurrency (>1000 TPS), this
      may become a bottleneck. Monitor with <literal>pg_stat_activity</literal>
      wait events.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>No TOAST Integration</emphasis>: RECNO tables cannot use
      standard TOAST. All large values use RECNO overflow pages, which
      may have different performance characteristics.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-limitations-workarounds">
   <title>Workarounds and Best Practices</title>

   <para>
    Until these limitations are addressed, consider these workarounds:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Index Bloat Management</emphasis>: Schedule regular
      <command>REINDEX CONCURRENTLY</command> for frequently updated tables:
     </para>
<programlisting>
-- Weekly reindex for high-update tables
SELECT cron.schedule('reindex-recno-tables', '0 2 * * 0',
  $$REINDEX TABLE CONCURRENTLY customers;$$);
</programlisting>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Optimize for Missing Index-Only Scans</emphasis>: Use
      covering indexes to minimize heap fetches:
     </para>
<programlisting>
-- Instead of: CREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id);
-- Use: CREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (order_date, total);
</programlisting>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Handle Foreign Key Conflicts</emphasis>: Use advisory locks
      for critical FK operations:
     </para>
<programlisting>
-- Wrap FK-heavy operations in advisory locks
SELECT pg_advisory_lock(12345);
-- Perform FK operations
SELECT pg_advisory_unlock(12345);
</programlisting>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-replication">
  <title>Replication Compatibility</title>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>replication</primary>
   <secondary>RECNO</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   RECNO supports both physical replication (streaming replication) and logical
   replication, maintaining full compatibility with
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>'s replication infrastructure.  This
   section documents the supported scenarios, configuration requirements, and
   known limitations.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-replication-physical">
   <title>Physical Replication (Streaming Replication)</title>

   <para>
    Physical replication replicates all WAL records from a primary server to one
    or more standby servers, creating exact byte-for-byte copies of the database.
    RECNO tables are fully supported in physical replication setups.
   </para>

   <sect3 id="recno-replication-physical-default">
    <title>Default Timestamp Mode</title>

    <para>
     When <varname>recno_use_hlc</varname> is <literal>off</literal> (the
     default), RECNO uses monotonic timestamps generated from the system clock.
     Physical replication works identically to heap tables.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Configuration:</emphasis> No special configuration required.
     Standard streaming replication setup applies.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Timestamp behavior:</emphasis> The standby server replays WAL
     records and adopts the primary's commit timestamps. The standby's RECNO
     timestamp counter is advanced to match the primary's timestamps.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Testing status:</emphasis> Fully tested and production-ready.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Known limitations:</emphasis> None. RECNO tables replicate
     identically to heap tables.
    </para>
   </sect3>

   <sect3 id="recno-replication-physical-hlc">
    <title>HLC Mode</title>

    <para>
     When <varname>recno_use_hlc</varname> is <literal>on</literal>, RECNO
     uses Hybrid Logical Clocks for commit timestamps. Physical replication
     requires additional configuration to maintain causal consistency.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Configuration:</emphasis>
    </para>

    <orderedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Set <varname>recno_use_hlc = on</varname> on both primary and standby
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Configure <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> to match the actual
       maximum clock skew between nodes (default: 500ms, suitable for
       NTP-synchronized clusters)
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Optionally set <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname>:
       <itemizedlist>
        <listitem>
         <para>
          <literal>on</literal>: Standby waits for physical clock to pass
          uncertainty intervals before serving reads (stronger consistency,
          higher latency)
         </para>
        </listitem>
        <listitem>
         <para>
          <literal>off</literal> (default): Standby fast-forwards HLC past
          uncertainty intervals (lower latency, still causally consistent)
         </para>
        </listitem>
       </itemizedlist>
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </orderedlist>

    <para>
     <emphasis>HLC advancement on standby:</emphasis> When the standby replays a
     WAL record containing <structname>xl_recno_hlc_info</structname>, it:
    </para>

    <orderedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Extracts the primary's <structfield>commit_hlc</structfield>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Advances its local HLC to at least <structfield>commit_hlc</structfield>
       (maintaining causal consistency)
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       If <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname> is <literal>on</literal>,
       waits until the physical clock passes
       <structfield>uncertainty_upper</structfield> before proceeding
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </orderedlist>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Testing status:</emphasis> Tested with single standby and cascading
     replication. Multi-standby topologies are supported but less extensively tested.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Known limitations:</emphasis>
    </para>

    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Clock skew between primary and standby must not exceed
       <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname>. Exceeding this bound may cause
       ordering anomalies.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       If the standby's clock is significantly behind the primary's, uncertainty
       waiting may introduce replication lag (up to
       <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> milliseconds per transaction).
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Best practices:</emphasis>
    </para>

    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Use NTP or chrony to synchronize clocks across all nodes
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Monitor clock skew with <literal>SELECT recno_clock_skew_stats()</literal>
       on standby servers
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Set <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> conservatively based on
       observed maximum skew (add 20-50% safety margin)
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
   </sect3>

   <sect3 id="recno-replication-standby-promotion">
    <title>Standby Promotion</title>

    <para>
     When a standby is promoted to primary:
    </para>

    <orderedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       The standby's current HLC or timestamp counter becomes the new baseline
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       New commits continue from the last replayed timestamp, ensuring monotonicity
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       No special action required; promotion works identically to heap tables
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </orderedlist>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Testing status:</emphasis> Tested for default mode and HLC mode.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Known limitations:</emphasis> None.
    </para>
   </sect3>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-replication-logical">
   <title>Logical Replication</title>

   <para>
    Logical replication replicates changes at the SQL level, allowing selective
    replication of tables and schema-independent replication between different
    <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> versions. RECNO supports logical
    replication through a dedicated WAL decoding function.
   </para>

   <sect3 id="recno-replication-logical-publisher">
    <title>As Publisher (Primary with RECNO Tables)</title>

    <para>
     A RECNO table can be published for logical replication. Changes to RECNO
     tuples are decoded from RECNO WAL records into logical replication messages.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Configuration:</emphasis>
    </para>

    <orderedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Set <varname>wal_level = logical</varname>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Create a publication: <literal>CREATE PUBLICATION pub FOR TABLE recno_table;</literal>
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Create a subscription on the subscriber: <literal>CREATE SUBSCRIPTION sub CONNECTION 'host=...' PUBLICATION pub;</literal>
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </orderedlist>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Decoding behavior:</emphasis> RECNO's logical decoding function
     (<function>recno_decode</function>) translates RECNO WAL records as follows:
    </para>

    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <literal>XLOG_RECNO_INSERT</literal> → logical INSERT
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <literal>XLOG_RECNO_UPDATE_INPLACE</literal> → logical UPDATE (includes old
       and new tuple)
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <literal>XLOG_RECNO_DELETE</literal> → logical DELETE
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Other RECNO-specific records (defrag, compress, overflow) are not decoded
       (they are internal optimizations invisible to logical replication)
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>

    <para>
     <emphasis>HLC propagation:</emphasis> If <varname>recno_use_hlc</varname>
     is <literal>on</literal>, the <structname>xl_recno_hlc_info</structname>
     is included in the logical replication stream as a custom message. A
     subscriber that also uses RECNO in HLC mode can consume this data to
     maintain causal consistency.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Testing status:</emphasis> Tested with basic DML operations.
     Comprehensive testing with large transactions and concurrent DML pending.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Known limitations:</emphasis>
    </para>

    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Initial table synchronization (COPY) may not preserve RECNO's compression
       or overflow structure if the subscriber uses heap tables. The logical data
       is correct, but storage optimizations are not replicated.
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       RECNO's in-place updates mean that UPDATE WAL records include both old and
       new tuple images. This increases WAL volume compared to heap (which logs
       only new tuple for updates).
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
   </sect3>

   <sect3 id="recno-replication-logical-subscriber">
    <title>As Subscriber (Replica with RECNO Tables)</title>

    <para>
     A RECNO table can subscribe to changes from any
     <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> table (heap or RECNO).
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Configuration:</emphasis> Same as standard logical replication.
     The subscriber's RECNO table receives INSERTs, UPDATEs, and DELETEs decoded
     from the publisher's WAL.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Storage behavior:</emphasis> Changes replicated into a RECNO
     subscriber table benefit from RECNO's in-place updates and compression,
     reducing storage bloat compared to a heap subscriber.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>HLC handling:</emphasis> If the subscriber uses
     <varname>recno_use_hlc = on</varname> and the publisher includes HLC
     metadata, the subscriber advances its local HLC based on incoming commit
     timestamps.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Testing status:</emphasis> Tested with basic DML operations.
    </para>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Known limitations:</emphasis>
    </para>

    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       Logical replication applies changes in commit order. If the subscriber's
       local clock is ahead of the publisher's commit timestamps, timestamp-based
       queries on the subscriber may observe unexpected ordering.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>
   </sect3>

   <sect3 id="recno-replication-logical-version-compat">
    <title>Version Compatibility</title>

    <para>
     Logical replication between different <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>
     versions is supported:
    </para>

    <itemizedlist>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       RECNO tables can replicate to/from heap tables (different storage, same
       logical data)
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       RECNO on PostgreSQL 17+ can replicate to/from RECNO on PostgreSQL 17+ (if
       both have RECNO extension installed)
      </para>
     </listitem>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       RECNO cannot replicate to PostgreSQL versions without RECNO support if the
       subscriber creates a RECNO table (subscriber must use heap)
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </itemizedlist>

    <para>
     <emphasis>Testing status:</emphasis> Same-version replication tested.
     Cross-version testing (RECNO → heap) partially tested.
    </para>
   </sect3>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-replication-matrix">
   <title>Compatibility Matrix</title>

   <para>
    The following table summarizes replication scenarios and their support status:
   </para>

   <table id="recno-replication-matrix-table">
    <title>RECNO Replication Compatibility Matrix</title>
    <tgroup cols="4">
     <thead>
      <row>
       <entry>Scenario</entry>
       <entry>Supported</entry>
       <entry>Configuration Notes</entry>
       <entry>Limitations</entry>
      </row>
     </thead>
     <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry>Physical replication, default mode</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Standard setup, no special config</entry>
       <entry>None</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Physical replication, HLC mode</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Set <varname>recno_use_hlc=on</varname>, configure clock skew bounds</entry>
       <entry>Requires clock synchronization (NTP)</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Cascading replication</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Standard cascading setup</entry>
       <entry>HLC uncertainty may accumulate across cascades</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Standby promotion</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Standard promotion procedure</entry>
       <entry>None</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Logical replication: RECNO → heap</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Standard logical replication setup</entry>
       <entry>RECNO optimizations (compression, in-place updates) not replicated</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Logical replication: heap → RECNO</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Standard logical replication setup</entry>
       <entry>None</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Logical replication: RECNO → RECNO</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Optionally enable HLC propagation for causal consistency</entry>
       <entry>HLC metadata propagation requires custom handling</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Bidirectional logical replication</entry>
       <entry>⚠️ Experimental</entry>
       <entry>Use HLC mode with conflict resolution</entry>
       <entry>Conflict resolution not fully implemented</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Synchronous replication</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>Standard <varname>synchronous_commit</varname> settings</entry>
       <entry>HLC uncertainty waiting may increase commit latency</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Logical decoding to third-party tools</entry>
       <entry>✅ Yes</entry>
       <entry>RECNO changes appear as standard INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE</entry>
       <entry>Third-party tools unaware of RECNO-specific WAL records</entry>
      </row>
     </tbody>
    </tgroup>
   </table>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-replication-clock-sync">
   <title>Clock Synchronization Requirements</title>

   <para>
    RECNO's timestamp-based MVCC means clock synchronization quality directly
    affects replication correctness and performance.  The following table
    summarizes requirements by deployment topology:
   </para>

   <table id="recno-clock-sync-table">
    <title>Clock Synchronization Requirements by Topology</title>
    <tgroup cols="5">
     <thead>
      <row>
       <entry>Topology</entry>
       <entry>Sync Method</entry>
       <entry>Target Accuracy</entry>
       <entry><varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname></entry>
       <entry>Notes</entry>
      </row>
     </thead>
     <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry>Single node</entry>
       <entry>None required</entry>
       <entry>N/A</entry>
       <entry>Default (500)</entry>
       <entry>Monotonic counter ensures correctness on one host</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Primary + standby (default mode)</entry>
       <entry>NTP</entry>
       <entry>&lt;100 ms</entry>
       <entry>200</entry>
       <entry>Standby replays WAL; timestamps come from primary</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Primary + standby (HLC mode)</entry>
       <entry>NTP or chrony</entry>
       <entry>&lt;50 ms</entry>
       <entry>100</entry>
       <entry>HLC advances locally on standby; tighter sync reduces uncertainty wait</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Cascading standbys (HLC mode)</entry>
       <entry>NTP or chrony</entry>
       <entry>&lt;50 ms</entry>
       <entry>150</entry>
       <entry>Uncertainty accumulates across cascade levels; add margin</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Logical replication (same datacenter)</entry>
       <entry>NTP with local stratum-1</entry>
       <entry>&lt;10 ms</entry>
       <entry>50</entry>
       <entry>Independent clocks on publisher and subscriber</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Logical replication (cross-datacenter)</entry>
       <entry>PTP or chrony with hardware timestamping</entry>
       <entry>&lt;5 ms</entry>
       <entry>20</entry>
       <entry>WAN latency makes tight sync more important</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Bidirectional logical replication</entry>
       <entry>PTP or AWS ClockBound</entry>
       <entry>&lt;1 ms</entry>
       <entry>5</entry>
       <entry>Conflict resolution depends on accurate ordering</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>AWS EC2 with ClockBound</entry>
       <entry>ClockBound daemon</entry>
       <entry>&lt;1 ms</entry>
       <entry>1</entry>
       <entry>ClockBound provides proven sub-millisecond error bounds</entry>
      </row>
     </tbody>
    </tgroup>
   </table>

   <para>
    The <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> value should be set to at
    least 2&times; the observed maximum clock skew in your environment.  Monitor
    actual skew using <literal>chronyc tracking</literal> or the
    <function>recno_clock_skew_stats()</function> function, and adjust
    accordingly.
   </para>

   <para>
    When <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait</varname> is <literal>on</literal>,
    the value of <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> directly
    determines the maximum additional read latency a replica may incur
    when encountering a recently committed tuple from another node.
    Tighter clock synchronization allows a smaller offset, reducing
    this latency.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-replication-best-practices">
   <title>Best Practices</title>

   <para>
    For production deployments with replication:
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Clock synchronization:</emphasis> Use NTP or chrony on all nodes,
      especially when using HLC mode. Aim for &lt;50ms clock skew.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Monitor replication lag:</emphasis> Use standard PostgreSQL
      replication monitoring (e.g., <function>pg_stat_replication</function>,
      <function>pg_replication_slots</function>) as with heap tables.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Test failover scenarios:</emphasis> Practice standby promotion
      with RECNO tables to ensure smooth failovers.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Logical replication:</emphasis> If replicating from RECNO to heap
      for compatibility reasons, ensure the subscriber has sufficient storage for
      heap's tuple versioning overhead.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>HLC mode in multi-site deployments:</emphasis> Configure
      <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> based on measured WAN latency
      and clock drift. Add a safety margin (e.g., if max observed skew is 200ms,
      set to 300ms).
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-replication-troubleshooting">
   <title>Troubleshooting</title>

   <para>
    Common replication issues with RECNO:
   </para>

   <variablelist>
    <varlistentry>
     <term>Standby reports "HLC timestamp anomaly"</term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Cause:</emphasis> Clock skew exceeds
       <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname>.
      </para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> Synchronize clocks with NTP and increase
       <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> if needed.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>Logical replication subscriber fails with "unknown WAL record type"</term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Cause:</emphasis> Subscriber's PostgreSQL version doesn't have
       RECNO support, and publisher is sending RECNO-specific records.
      </para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> Ensure both publisher and subscriber have
       RECNO installed, or replicate to heap tables on subscriber.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>

    <varlistentry>
     <term>High replication lag with HLC mode and <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait=on</varname></term>
     <listitem>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Cause:</emphasis> Standby waiting for physical clock to pass
       uncertainty intervals.
      </para>
      <para>
       <emphasis>Solution:</emphasis> Set <varname>recno_uncertainty_wait=off</varname>
       for lower latency (still maintains causal consistency), or reduce
       <varname>recno_max_clock_offset_ms</varname> if clocks are well-synchronized.
      </para>
     </listitem>
    </varlistentry>
   </variablelist>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="recno-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   This section demonstrates common usage patterns.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="recno-example-oltp">
   <title>OLTP Application Table</title>

<programlisting>
-- User accounts with frequent profile updates
CREATE TABLE accounts (
    id         bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
    username   text UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    email      text NOT NULL,
    profile    jsonb DEFAULT '{}',
    balance    numeric(12,2) DEFAULT 0,
    updated_at timestamptz DEFAULT now()
) USING recno;

CREATE INDEX ON accounts (email);
CREATE INDEX ON accounts USING gin (profile);

-- In-place update (profile change fits in existing space)
UPDATE accounts SET profile = profile || '{"verified": true}'::jsonb,
                    updated_at = now()
WHERE id = 42;
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-example-documents">
   <title>Document Storage</title>

<programlisting>
-- Documents with large content using overflow pages
CREATE TABLE articles (
    id         serial PRIMARY KEY,
    title      text NOT NULL,
    body       text,        -- large values use overflow pages
    metadata   jsonb,
    created_at timestamptz DEFAULT now()
) USING recno;

-- Text search on body column works normally
CREATE INDEX ON articles USING gin (to_tsvector('english', body));
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-example-monitoring">
   <title>Monitoring Compression Effectiveness</title>

<programlisting>
-- Check if compression is providing benefit
SELECT * FROM recno_compression_stats('articles');

-- Compare RECNO table size to estimated uncompressed size
SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('articles')) AS current_size;

-- Review space management state
SELECT * FROM recno_fsm_stats('articles');
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="recno-example-tuning">
   <title>Performance Tuning</title>

<programlisting>
-- For update-heavy workloads: keep more free space for in-place updates
ALTER SYSTEM SET recno_defrag_threshold = 0.5;

-- For read-heavy workloads with large text: increase compression
ALTER SYSTEM SET recno_compression_level = 6;

-- For high concurrency without serializable requirements
ALTER SYSTEM SET recno_enable_serializable = off;
SELECT pg_reload_conf();
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

</chapter>
